# What the program's C is compiled under, a unit's compile and a probe's
# alike, and what the program's headers resolve to under it (design.md
# §4.19, §1.12; defects 163, 167 and 168, 2026-10-02).
#
# **A probe judges the header the program's own compile will see, with the
# words that compile sees it with.** The pointee check, the layout check and
# a refused round's questions each compile the program's headers before any
# unit does (`cli/pointee.hero`, `cli/layout.hero`, `cli/header_ask.hero`).
# A unit compiles under `unit_words`: the compiler's flags, the build's level and
# sanitizers, and the search, in the order a unit's compile searches it
# (`search_words`); `cli/units.hero` builds its line from that list. A probe
# compiles under the same list but its diagnostics (`probe_words`), which
# decide whether a unit compiles and never what a type is (defect 163). Until
# defect 167 a probe's list was the flags and the search alone, with no level
# and no sanitizer, so a header that types a parameter or lays out a struct
# otherwise under `__OPTIMIZE__` or `__has_feature(address_sanitizer)` was
# judged as `-O0` reads it while the program was compiled at `-O2`, the level
# `heroes run` builds at by default, or under `--sanitize`: measured
# 2026-10-02, an `@p: i32` over `int64_t *` built at exit 0 and C wrote eight
# bytes into the four, which ASan reported, and a record leaving out a member
# the header adds when optimising built and printed a wrong value.
#
# **And a verdict a probe keeps is keyed by everything it depends on**
# (defect 168): every word of its lines, from the one list the lines are
# built from (`probe_words`), and what the headers resolve to and hold under
# those words, asked of clang once in each round of a build for both checks
# (`reads`, `probe`). Until then the layout check's key named the screen's
# text, the `--include` directories, a package's compile words and the
# probe's flags, and the pointee check's the declaring file's name: neither
# named the source's own directory, so a program beside a same-named header
# replayed another directory's verdict, measured 2026-10-02 both ways on one
# `build/` (a record leaving a member out built at exit 0 and printed a
# wrong value; the correct program beside the other header stopped at exit
# 2), and the library's own asks, whose declaring file is the same in every
# program, replayed a refusal onto a program whose header refutes nothing.
# A kept record (`cli/deps`) vouches only for the files a run READ, so a
# header that appeared earlier in the search order is in no record: the
# same program, its own source edited, compiled against the new header
# while the layout check replayed the old one's verdict, exit 0 and a
# wrong value, measured the same day with `--include`. `reads` asks what a
# record cannot, which files these words make of these includes now.

use cli/deps
use cli/digest
use cli/flags
use cli/libraries
use cli/opening
use cli/order_trial
use cli/package_reads
use cli/process
use cli/publish
use cli/response_file
use cli/strings
use emit/c_text
use emit/clang_place

# Everything the program's C is compiled under but the words of one run (its
# input, its output, its listing): the level, whether it is sanitised, the
# directories the program names with `--include`, a package's answer, the
# runtime, and the source's own directory.
record Compiling
    level: str
    sanitize: bool
    search_include: [str]
    package_flags: [str]
    runtime: str
    source_dir: str

# Every word a unit of the program compiles under but its run's own: the
# compiler's flags, the level, its debug word and the sanitizers' flags
# (`flags.level_words`), then the search. `units.tu_object` builds its line
# from this list and names it in its key, and every probe takes it but its
# diagnostics, so no word reaches the program's compile without reaching every
# probe of its headers.
function unit_words(c: Compiling) -> [str]
    return strings.with_all(list: flags.level_words(level: c.level, sanitize: c.sanitize), more: search_words(c))

# The search, in the order a unit's compile searches (`units.tu_object`): the
# runtime first, then the `--include` directories, a package's compile words
# by the one rule every compile step reads (`libraries.compile_flags`; defect
# 161: `-I` alone was kept here), and the source's own directory, so `extern
# "mylib.h"` finds a header beside the `.hero` file. The same headers must be
# found from the same places, or the question is asked of another header.
#
# **The runtime's directory is the first a unit searches** (panel 235's R9,
# its C1; defect 2324, 2026-11-07), so no file of the program's stands in for
# a header the C this compiler writes includes, `heroes_unit.h`, `hero_os.h`,
# the guard's two and `heroes_standard.h`: each is the first the unit names
# and is found where `-I` is searched first. Until then the runtime stood
# after the `--include` directories and a package's `-I`, and a
# `heroes_standard.h` in a directory `--include` named was read in its place,
# read the runtime's through `#include_next` and defined
# `__builtin_add_overflow` to report nothing: an `i64` sum past the top
# printed `-9223372036854775808` at exit 0 where the program panics, exit 134,
# and nothing was said. The rest keep their order, so a program's header found
# in an `--include` directory before its own root is still found there.
function search_words(c: Compiling) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= ["-I", c.runtime]

    for one in c.search_include
        out @ strings.with_all(list: out, more: ["-I", one])

    for flag in libraries.compile_flags(c.package_flags)
        out @ out.push(flag)

    if c.source_dir.len() > 0
        out @ strings.with_all(list: out, more: ["-I", c.source_dir])

    return out

# **A fetched package's unit compiles under its own root** (panel 235's R9,
# its C1, ratified 2026-11-07): the words a unit of the program compiles
# under, with no `--include` directory, which is the program's flag and
# none of the package's, and the package's root where the program's root
# stands, so its search is the runtime's directory, the package's own
# `pkgconfig` groups' answer, which `under` already holds narrowed to the
# unit's headers (`settled.unit_words`), and its root, and nothing of the
# program's or of another package. A header the package ships beside its
# modules is found there without `--include`, a file at its root named
# like the runtime's is never read, and no file of the program's stands in
# for one of the package's. `root` is where the package's tree is placed:
# `package_units.unit_under` passes it for each unit of a fetched module, and
# every probe of its headers takes the same words (`probing.probings_of`),
# since defect 2351; until then no build called this, and every unit took
# the program's words (`settled.compiled_under`).
function of_package(under: Compiling, root: str) -> Compiling
    none: [str] = []
    return Compiling(
        level: under.level,
        sanitize: under.sanitize,
        search_include: none,
        package_flags: under.package_flags,
        runtime: under.runtime,
        source_dir: root
    )

# **What a fetched package's unit read lies where a package may read** (panel
# 235's R9, its C2; `cli/package_reads.hero`): `listing` is a `-M` listing
# its words made, a probe's (`reads`) or its opening's (`unit_reach`), judged
# before anything compiles; `under` is the unit's own (`of_package`) and
# `root` the package's, which `under` searches as its own folder or, where
# the unit reaches another package's header, among its `-I` words with that
# package's after it (`package_units.unit_under`, defect 2357), each of them
# a folder it may read. The toolchain's own directories are asked of clang
# under the unit's flags and no search word.
function within_root(build: str, under: Compiling, root: str, listing: str?) -> ()?
    own = package_reads.toolchain_own(build: build, words: flags.level_words(level: under.level, sanitize: under.sanitize))?
    return package_reads.judged(
        own: own,
        root: root,
        runtime: under.runtime,
        package_flags: libraries.compile_flags(under.package_flags),
        listing: listing
    )

# Every word a probe's line compiles under but its own run's: a unit's
# words (`unit_words`) but their diagnostics. Each probe builds its lines from
# this list and names it in its key, so no word reaches a line without
# reaching the key.
function probe_words(c: Compiling) -> [str]
    return without_diagnostics(unit_words(c))

# What one round's two checks ask with (`assemble.round`): the words their
# lines compile under, the headers every one of their units opens with, what
# those headers resolve to under those words (`reads`), and the digests that
# answer took, so a check vouching for its record hashes no file twice. Each
# check takes its headers from here, so the listing its key names is the
# listing of the includes its units open with, and one preprocessing run
# serves both checks.
record Probe
    words: [str]
    headers: [str]
    read: str?
    digests: {str: str}

function probe(build: str, under: Compiling, headers: [str]) -> Probe
    digests: {str: str} @= {}
    return probe_with(build, under: under, headers: headers, memo: @digests)

# The same with the build's memo of what it hashed already, which the probes
# of each module's headers share (`cli/probing.hero`).
function probe_with(build: str, under: Compiling, headers: [str], @memo: {str: str}) -> Probe
    words = probe_words(under)
    read = reads(build, words: words, headers: headers, @memo)
    return Probe(words: words, headers: headers, read: read, digests: memo)

# The compiler's own flags a probe compiles under, so it reads a header as
# the program's compile does (defect 163, 2026-10-02): every flag of
# `flags.flags()` but its diagnostics, one rule and never a second list.
# Measured with this Mac's clang 21, preprocessing for the four legs'
# targets: of the sixteen the list held then, `-std=gnu11` (against clang's
# own gnu17), the two `-D`s and, on Linux arm64 alone, `-fsigned-char` change
# what C means by a predefined macro; `-g` and the two code-generation flags
# change none and are kept, so a flag that changes what a type is without a
# macro is never left out (since panel 197 `-g` is the level's, and reaches a
# probe with the level, through `unit_words`); the diagnostics change none either and decide whether a unit
# compiles, not what a type is, so a probe never fails on a header's
# warning. The probes compiled under `-std=gnu11` alone until then, so on
# Linux arm64, where a plain `char` is unsigned by default, the pointee
# check refused the `@p: i8` that the program's own compile, under
# `-fsigned-char`, agrees with, and accepted the `@p: u8` it refuses.
function probe_flags() -> [str]
    return without_diagnostics(flags.flags())

# The words but the diagnostics, `-W` and every `-Werror=`: the one rule both
# lists above are made by.
function without_diagnostics(listed: [str]) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= []

    for word in listed
        if !strings.starts_with(text: word, prefix: "-W")
            out @ out.push(word)

    return out

# What the headers resolve to under `words`, and what each one holds: the
# rows of `cli/deps`, `<digest> <path>` per file, from one `clang -M` over
# the lines every probe unit opens with (`include_lines`), so the files are
# the ones the probe's own run would read, found from the same places.
# Absent where the headers do not preprocess: the build's own compile
# reports that on the author's line, as each probe leaves a run that fails.
# One preprocessing run, measured 2026-10-02 on this Mac at a load of 2.6:
# ten of them over the four headers of a one-record program took 0.10 s of
# user time, as ten of the layout check's screen runs did, against 0.21 s
# for that program's whole warm build.
function reads(build: str, words: [str], headers: [str], @memo: {str: str}) -> str?
    return reads_text(build: build, words: words, text: include_lines(headers), @memo)

# What `text`, the lines a unit opens with, reaches under `words`: one `clang
# -M` over it, written under `build/reads/` and named by its text
# (`opening.reached`), its own file no row.
function reads_text(build: str, words: [str], text: str, @memo: {str: str}) -> str?
    dir = build + "/reads"
    process.mkdir_all(dir)?
    unit = dir + "/reads-" + digest.digest(text) + ".c"
    publish.write_whole(path: unit, text: text, keyed: true)?
    line = response_file.carried(line: strings.with_all(list: ["clang"], more: words), dir: dir)
    none: [str] = []
    return opening.reached(
        line: line,
        input: unit,
        depfile: publish.private_name(dir + "/reads.d"),
        skip: dir + "/",
        keyed: none,
        @memo
    )

# What `head`, a unit's opening (`opening.kept_of`, defect 2289), reaches
# under the words the unit compiles under: asked of clang once in a build for
# each opening and words, `@opened` holding each answer by the two, or
# `opening.UNREAD` where clang gave none. The units of a program's plain
# modules share one opening, so a hundred of them ask once.
function unit_reach(build: str, words: [str], head: str, @memo: {str: str}, @opened: {str: str}) -> str?
    key = libraries.key_text(words) + "|" + head
    known = opened[key]

    if !known.is_err() && known.must() != opening.UNREAD
        return known

    if !known.is_err()
        return fail(code: "unread", msg: "the includes did not preprocess")
    got = reads_text(build: build, words: words, text: head, @memo)
    opened[key] @ got.default(opening.UNREAD)
    return got

# The lines every probe unit opens with: one angled include per header, in
# the program's own order (`emit/externs.headers`), spelled as the program's
# own unit spells them (`emit/c_text.hero`).
function include_lines(headers: [str]) -> str
    return c_text.includes(headers).join("\n") + "\n"

## Tests

# **What the headers resolve to is clang's answer, and it moves when a header
# appears earlier in the search** (defect 168). The source's directory holds
# `x.h`; an `--include` directory searched before it holds nothing, then the
# same name: the same words and the same includes now read another file, and
# the answer says so, by path and by what it holds, in one row. **The rows
# are read with every separator a `/`** (defect 214, 2026-10-03): clang on
# Windows writes every one of a listing's separators as `\`, those it was
# given as `/` among them, so the paths written here with `/` were never in
# the answer there and the first assertion failed while the second, that
# the answer does not hold the other directory's `x.h`, passed whatever
# `reads` answered. The turn is written in the test, as
# `emit/clang_place.slashed` writes it for clang's diagnostics, because a
# `use` line would move every function of this module down one line in the
# seed's `#line` directives, measured: the same seed byte for byte but them.
# **And the probe's own unit is never a row**: it lives in the directory the
# key names, which `skip` leaves out, and on Windows no row was left out
# until `deps.under` read a `\` and a `/` as one separator (defect 214's
# second half, 2026-10-03).
test "what a probe's headers resolve to is asked of clang, and a header that appears earlier in the search is another answer (defect 168)"
    root = "build/selftest-compiling-reads"
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(root + "/inc").is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(root + "/src").is_err()
    one = "#define HERO_READS 1\n"
    two = "#define HERO_READS 2\n"
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/src/x.h", text: one).is_err()
    c = Compiling(
        level: "-O0",
        sanitize: false,
        search_include: [root + "/inc"],
        package_flags: [],
        runtime: "runtime",
        source_dir: root + "/src"
    )
    words = probe_words(c)
    first: {str: str} @= {}
    turned: [str] @= []

    for ch in reads(root + "/build", words: words, headers: ["x.h"], @first).must().chars()
        if ch == "\\"
            turned @ turned.push("/")
        else
            turned @ turned.push(ch)

    beside = turned.join("")
    assert strings.holds(text: beside, needle: digest.digest(one) + " " + root + "/src/x.h")
    assert !strings.holds(text: beside, needle: root + "/inc/x.h")
    assert !strings.holds(text: beside, needle: root + "/build/reads/")
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/inc/x.h", text: two).is_err()
    second: {str: str} @= {}
    again: [str] @= []

    for ch in reads(root + "/build", words: words, headers: ["x.h"], @second).must().chars()
        if ch == "\\"
            again @ again.push("/")
        else
            again @ again.push(ch)

    earlier = again.join("")
    assert strings.holds(text: earlier, needle: digest.digest(two) + " " + root + "/inc/x.h")
    assert !strings.holds(text: earlier, needle: root + "/src/x.h")
    assert !strings.holds(text: earlier, needle: root + "/build/reads/")
    assert earlier != beside

    # A header that is not there does not preprocess, and the answer is
    # absent rather than a listing of what was found.
    third: {str: str} @= {}
    assert reads(root + "/build", words: words, headers: ["hero-no-such-header.h"], @third).is_err()

# **No file of the program's stands in for a header the unit includes from the
# runtime** (defect 2324). A directory `--include` names, a package's `-I` and
# the source's own directory each hold a file named like every header of the
# runtime's, each an `#error`; the `--include` directory also holds a header
# of the program's own with a name no runtime header has, and one named like
# the runtime's under a folder of its own. The unit's opening as the emitter
# writes it (`order_trial.opening`), with groups naming those two and the
# runtime's `hero_compiler.h`, reads every runtime header from the runtime and
# the program's two from where they stand, and no planted file. Under the
# order that stood until the repair, the `--include` directory ahead of the
# runtime's, the same opening reads a planted file and does not preprocess,
# so the test is not blind.
test "a unit's opening reads the runtime's own headers, whatever a directory of the program's holds (defect 2324)"
    root = "build/selftest-compiling-runtime-first"
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()
    planted = [
        "heroes_unit.h"
        "heroes_runtime.h"
        "hero_os.h"
        "heroes_guard_open.h"
        "heroes_guard_close.h"
        "heroes_standard.h"
        "hero_compiler.h"
    ]

    for dir in ["/inc/sub", "/pkg", "/src"]
        assert !process.mkdir_all(root + dir).is_err()

    for dir in ["/inc", "/pkg", "/src"]
        for name in planted
            assert !write_file(path: root + dir + "/" + name, text: "#error a program's " + name + " was read in place of the runtime's\n").is_err()

    assert !write_file(path: root + "/inc/mine.h", text: "#define HERO_MINE 2324\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/inc/sub/heroes_standard.h", text: "#define HERO_SUB 2324\n").is_err()
    c = Compiling(
        level: "-O0",
        sanitize: false,
        search_include: [root + "/inc"],
        package_flags: ["-I" + root + "/pkg"],
        runtime: "runtime",
        source_dir: root + "/src"
    )
    text = order_trial.opening(["hero_compiler.h", "mine.h", "sub/heroes_standard.h"], question: "")
    memo: {str: str} @= {}
    read = reads_text(build: root + "/build", words: probe_words(c), text: text, @memo)
    assert !read.is_err()
    paths: [str] @= []

    # A row is `<digest> <path>`, after the digest of them all.
    for row in deps.lines(clang_place.slashed(read.must()))
        space: i64 @= 0

        while space < row.len() && row[space] != 32
            space @ space + 1

        if space < row.len()
            paths @ paths.push(row.slice(from: space + 1, to: row.len()))

    for name in planted
        mine: bool @= false

        for path in paths
            if path == "runtime/" + name
                mine @ true

        assert mine

    for path in paths
        if strings.starts_with(text: path, prefix: root + "/")
            assert path == root + "/inc/mine.h" || path == root + "/inc/sub/heroes_standard.h"

    assert strings.holds(text: paths.join("\n"), needle: root + "/inc/sub/heroes_standard.h")
    before = strings.with_all(list: ["-I", root + "/inc"], more: probe_words(c))
    assert reads_text(build: root + "/build", words: before, text: text, @memo).is_err()
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

# **A fetched package's unit searches the runtime and its own root, and none
# of the program's** (panel 235's R9, its C1): no `--include` directory, the
# package's root where the program's stood, its own packages' words kept.
test "a fetched package's unit searches the runtime first, then its own packages' words and its root, and nothing of the program's (panel 235's C1)"
    program = Compiling(
        level: "-O2",
        sanitize: false,
        search_include: ["inc"],
        package_flags: ["-DHERO_PKG", "-Ipkg"],
        runtime: "rt",
        source_dir: "src"
    )
    unit = of_package(program, root: "heroes.packages/vec")
    assert search_words(unit) == ["-I", "rt", "-DHERO_PKG", "-Ipkg", "-I", "heroes.packages/vec"]
    assert unit.level == "-O2"
    assert unit_words(unit) == strings.with_all(list: flags.level_words(level: "-O2", sanitize: false), more: search_words(unit))

# **The package's own header is read from its root, a file at its root
# named like the runtime's is not, and the program's are never searched**
# (C1), asked of clang as the unit's opening reads them: the program's
# `--include` directory and root each hold a `vecmath.h` that is an
# `#error`, the package's root holds the real one beside a planted
# `heroes_standard.h`; and what the opening read lies where a package may
# read (C2), the toolchain's own `stdint.h` among it.
test "a fetched package's unit reads its own header and the runtime's, never the program's (panel 235's C1 and C2)"
    root = "build/selftest-compiling-package-root"
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

    for dir in ["/inc", "/src", "/pkg/vec"]
        assert !process.mkdir_all(root + dir).is_err()

    for dir in ["/inc", "/src"]
        assert !write_file(path: root + dir + "/vecmath.h", text: "#error the program's vecmath.h was read for the package's\n").is_err()

    assert !write_file(path: root + "/pkg/vec/vecmath.h", text: "#include <stdint.h>\nstatic inline int64_t vm_dot(void) { return 32; }\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/pkg/vec/heroes_standard.h", text: "#error a planted heroes_standard.h was read\n").is_err()
    program = Compiling(
        level: "-O0",
        sanitize: false,
        search_include: [root + "/inc"],
        package_flags: [],
        runtime: "runtime",
        source_dir: root + "/src"
    )
    unit = of_package(program, root: root + "/pkg/vec")
    memo: {str: str} @= {}
    read = reads_text(
        build: root + "/build",
        words: probe_words(unit),
        text: order_trial.opening(["vecmath.h"], question: ""),
        @memo
    )
    assert !read.is_err()
    listed = clang_place.slashed(read.must())
    assert strings.holds(text: listed, needle: " " + root + "/pkg/vec/vecmath.h")
    assert strings.holds(text: listed, needle: " runtime/heroes_standard.h")
    assert !strings.holds(text: listed, needle: root + "/inc/")
    assert !strings.holds(text: listed, needle: root + "/src/")
    assert !strings.holds(text: listed, needle: root + "/pkg/vec/heroes_standard.h")
    assert !within_root(build: root + "/build", under: unit, root: root + "/pkg/vec", listing: read).is_err()

    # The same opening as a unit of the program reads the program's header.
    assert reads_text(
        build: root + "/build",
        words: probe_words(program),
        text: order_trial.opening(["vecmath.h"], question: ""),
        @memo
    ).is_err()
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

# **A package's header reaching a file outside its root is refused with the
# path, before anything compiles** (C2): by a relative climb, by `#embed`
# and by `__has_include`, each a row of the `-M` listing; a header of the
# machine's that the package's own `pkgconfig` answer names passes.
test "a fetched package's header that reads a file outside its root is refused with its path (panel 235's C2)"
    root = "build/selftest-compiling-package-outside"
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

    for dir in ["/pkg/vec", "/machine"]
        assert !process.mkdir_all(root + dir).is_err()

    assert !write_file(path: root + "/secret.txt", text: "BUILD-MACHINE-SECRET\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/outside.h", text: "#define HERO_OUTSIDE 1\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/machine/ray.h", text: "#define HERO_RAY 1\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/pkg/vec/climb.h", text: "#include \"../../outside.h\"\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/pkg/vec/embed.h", text: "static const char hero_embedded[] = {\n#embed \"../../secret.txt\"\n};\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/pkg/vec/probe.h", text: "#if __has_include(\"../../outside.h\")\n#define HERO_FOUND 1\n#endif\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/pkg/vec/clean.h", text: "#include <stdint.h>\n#include <ray.h>\n").is_err()
    program = Compiling(
        level: "-O0",
        sanitize: false,
        search_include: [],
        package_flags: ["-I" + root + "/machine"],
        runtime: "runtime",
        source_dir: "."
    )
    unit = of_package(program, root: root + "/pkg/vec")
    memo: {str: str} @= {}

    # A clang with no `#embed` (Linux's 18) does not preprocess `embed.h`, and
    # a listing it could not make is refused as unread: refused either way.
    for pair in [["climb.h", "outside.h"], ["embed.h", "secret.txt"], ["probe.h", "outside.h"]]
        read = reads_text(
            build: root + "/build",
            words: probe_words(unit),
            text: order_trial.opening([pair[0]], question: ""),
            @memo
        )
        said = within_root(build: root + "/build", under: unit, root: root + "/pkg/vec", listing: read)
        assert said.is_err()

        code = match said
            .ok _  => ""
            .err e => e.code

        # The message tells the file with `/`, as it tells the root, on every
        # platform: the Windows box's listing writes `\` (defect 2382).
        msg = match said
            .ok _  => ""
            .err e => e.msg

        assert code == "package_reads_outside" && strings.holds(text: msg, needle: pair[1]) && !strings.holds(text: msg, needle: "\\") || code == "unread" && read.is_err()

    clean = reads_text(
        build: root + "/build",
        words: probe_words(unit),
        text: order_trial.opening(["clean.h"], question: ""),
        @memo
    )
    assert !within_root(build: root + "/build", under: unit, root: root + "/pkg/vec", listing: clean).is_err()
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

# **A unit and a probe compile under one list** (defects 163 and 167): a
# unit's words are the compiler's flags, the build's level and sanitizers,
# then the search, the runtime first (defect 2324), the `--include`
# directories, a package's compile words but its link words, and the
# source's own directory last; a probe's are the same list but its
# diagnostics, the level and the sanitizers included.
# **A probe whose words pass what one Windows line holds reads its headers**
# (defect 586): the package words a unit compiles under reach every probe's
# line too, and the probes run before any unit does, so a line held to
# 32,767 characters stopped the build at its first question.
test "a probe whose words pass what one Windows command line holds reads its headers, its words read from a file (defect 586)"
    root = "build/selftest-compiling-long-words"
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(root + "/src").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/src/long.h", text: "#if HEROES_LONG_LAST != 586\n#error the words were not read\n#endif\n").is_err()
    answer: [str] @= ["-DHEROES_LONG_LAST=586"]
    length: i64 @= 0

    while length <= 32767
        word = "-DHEROES_LONG_PAD_" + answer.len().to_str() + "_" + "0123456789".repeat(4) + "=1"
        answer @ answer.push(word)
        length @ length + word.len() + 1

    c = Compiling(
        level: "-O0",
        sanitize: false,
        search_include: [],
        package_flags: answer,
        runtime: "runtime",
        source_dir: root + "/src"
    )
    memo: {str: str} @= {}
    read = reads(root + "/build", words: probe_words(c), headers: ["long.h"], @memo)
    assert !read.is_err()
    assert strings.holds(text: read.must(), needle: "long.h")
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

test "a probe compiles under a unit's words but their diagnostics, the level and the sanitizers included (defect 167)"
    c = Compiling(
        level: "-O2",
        sanitize: true,
        search_include: ["inc"],
        package_flags: ["-DHERO_PKG", "-lz", "-Ipkg"],
        runtime: "rt",
        source_dir: "src"
    )
    assert search_words(c) == ["-I", "rt", "-I", "inc", "-DHERO_PKG", "-Ipkg", "-I", "src"]

    # The machine's frame word stands between the list and the level, where
    # this machine has one (defect 2253): Linux arm64 read this test red until
    # it was named here.
    listed = strings.with_all(list: flags.flags(), more: flags.frame_words())
    unit = strings.with_all(list: listed, more: ["-O2", "-gline-tables-only", "-fsanitize=address,undefined"])
    assert unit_words(c) == strings.with_all(list: unit, more: search_words(c))
    framed = strings.with_all(list: probe_flags(), more: flags.frame_words())
    probed = strings.with_all(list: framed, more: ["-O2", "-gline-tables-only", "-fsanitize=address,undefined"])
    assert probe_words(c) == strings.with_all(list: probed, more: search_words(c))
    none = Compiling(
        level: "-O0",
        sanitize: false,
        search_include: [],
        package_flags: [],
        runtime: "rt",
        source_dir: ""
    )
    assert search_words(none) == ["-I", "rt"]
    assert probe_words(none) == strings.with_all(list: framed, more: ["-O0", "-g", "-I", "rt"])

# **A probe reads a plain `char` as the program's compile does** (defect
# 163). The leg where the sign differs is Linux arm64, and clang answers for
# that target on any machine with no header: asked there, a plain `char` is
# unsigned under clang's own default and signed under the probe's flags, so
# the case reads the same on every leg, the arm64 container's included.
test "a probe's flags make a plain char signed for Linux arm64, as the program's compile does (defect 163)"
    assert !process.mkdir_all("build").is_err()
    unit = "build/selftest-probe-char.c"
    assert !write_file(path: unit, text: "_Static_assert((char)-1 < 0, \"a plain char is signed\");\n").is_err()
    target = "--target=aarch64-linux-gnu"
    bare = process.run(
        program: "clang",
        words: ["clang", target, "-fsyntax-only", unit],
        out: process.discard(),
        err: process.discard()
    )
    assert bare.started
    assert bare.code != 0
    probed: [str] @= strings.with_all(list: ["clang", target], more: probe_flags())
    probed @ strings.with_all(list: probed, more: ["-fsyntax-only", unit])
    signed = process.run(program: "clang", words: probed, out: process.discard(), err: process.discard())
    assert signed.code == 0

# **What a probe leaves out changes no predefined macro** (defect 163), asked
# of clang on this machine's own target and on Linux arm64's, the leg the
# rule was written for. A flag of `flags.flags()` that the rule dropped and
# that moved a macro would fail here, naming nothing but the comparison, and
# the sign of a plain `char` is shown to move one on that leg, so the
# comparison is not blind.
test "every flag a probe leaves out changes no predefined macro, here or on Linux arm64 (defect 163)"
    none: [str] = []
    arm = ["--target=aarch64-linux-gnu"]

    for target in [none, arm]
        base = macros(more: target)

        for flag in flags.flags()
            kept: bool @= false

            for one in probe_flags()
                if one == flag
                    kept @ true

            if !kept
                assert macros(more: target.push(flag)) == base

    assert macros(more: arm.push("-fsigned-char")) != macros(more: arm)

# **What the emitter writes of a program's text, clang reads as written and
# says nothing about** (defect 207), under the compiler's own flags: the
# comment that opens a unit, a header named with a `??` that ends a trigraph
# where the machine allows such a name (Windows refuses a `?` in one), a
# `#line` naming a file whose name holds the nine endings, a quote, a
# backslash and a comment's closer, and a literal holding them, whose bytes
# are the text's under `-std=c11`, where C reads trigraphs, as under the
# compiler's own `-std=gnu11`. The name clang reports is the name written,
# which every message mapped back to a `.hero` line and the debugger read.
#
# And a `#line` naming a file whose name holds characters of two, three and
# four bytes and a tab, and where a name may hold them, both line ends
# (defect 240): clang 23 refused the octal escapes such a name was written
# with until then, and read the trunk's `odd` as written, so this test
# passed there while `café.hero` did not build (2026-10-04).
test "clang reads the emitter's spellings of a program's text as written, and says nothing about them (defects 207 and 240)"
    root = "build/selftest-c-text"
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(root + "/inc").is_err()
    odd = "t??=??(??/??)??'??<??!??>??-a*/x\"y\\q.hero"
    wide: str @= "dé/€\t😀/p.hero"

    if process.exe_suffix() != ".exe"
        wide @ wide + "\r\n.hero"
    held = "what???) ??= ??( ??/ ??) ??' ??< ??! ??> ??- \"\\"
    bytes_of: [str] @= []
    i: i64 @= 0

    while i < held.len()
        bytes_of @ bytes_of.push(held[i].to_str())
        i @ i + 1

    unit: str @= "/* " + c_text.in_comment(odd) + " */\n#include <string.h>\n"

    if process.exe_suffix() != ".exe"
        assert !write_file(path: root + "/inc/h??).h", text: "#define HEROES_C_TEXT 1\n").is_err()
        unit @ unit + c_text.includes(["h??).h"]).join("\n") + "\n#if HEROES_C_TEXT != 1\n#error the header was not read\n#endif\n"
    unit @ unit + c_text.line_directive(at: 7, file: odd) + "\nint main(void) {\n" + c_text.line_directive(at: 9, file: wide) + "\n    static const char got[] = " + c_text.literal(held) + ";\n"
    unit @ unit + "    static const unsigned char want[] = {" + bytes_of.join(", ") + ", 0};\n"
    unit @ unit + "    return sizeof got != sizeof want || memcmp(got, want, sizeof want) != 0;\n}\n"
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/unit.c", text: unit).is_err()
    binary = root + "/unit" + process.exe_suffix()

    for dialect in ["-std=gnu11", "-std=c11"]
        words: [str] @= strings.with_all(list: ["clang"], more: flags.flags())
        words @ strings.with_all(list: words, more: [dialect, "-I", root + "/inc", root + "/unit.c", "-o", binary])
        built = process.run(program: "clang", words: words, out: process.discard(), err: root + "/said.txt")
        assert built.code == 0
        assert read_file(root + "/said.txt").must() == ""
        assert process.run(program: binary, words: [binary], out: process.discard(), err: process.discard()).code == 0

    # Each name, read back from clang's own diagnostic.
    for file in [odd, wide]
        assert !write_file(path: root + "/named.c", text: c_text.line_directive(at: 7, file: file) + "\n#error heroes-c-text\n").is_err()
        named: [str] @= strings.with_all(list: ["clang"], more: flags.flags())
        named @ strings.with_all(list: named, more: ["-fsyntax-only", root + "/named.c"])
        refused = process.run(program: "clang", words: named, out: process.discard(), err: root + "/said.txt")
        said = read_file(root + "/said.txt").must()
        assert refused.code != 0
        assert strings.holds(text: said, needle: file + ":7:")
        assert strings.holds(text: said, needle: "heroes-c-text")
        assert !strings.holds(text: said, needle: "warning:")

# What clang predefines for C under `more`, read from an empty unit.
function macros(more: [str]) -> str
    assert !process.mkdir_all("build").is_err()
    empty = "build/selftest-probe-empty.c"
    said = "build/selftest-probe-macros.txt"
    assert !write_file(path: empty, text: "").is_err()
    words: [str] @= strings.with_all(list: ["clang"], more: more)
    words @ strings.with_all(list: words, more: ["-E", "-dM", "-x", "c", empty])
    ran = process.run(program: "clang", words: words, out: said, err: process.discard())
    assert ran.code == 0
    return read_file(said).must()
